3073	                             C	; Ctrl-C handler.
3074	                             C	; "^C" and CR/LF is printed. Then the user registers are restored and the
3075	                             C	; user CTRL-C handler is executed. At this point the top of the stack has
3076	                             C	; 1) the interrupt return address should the user CTRL-C handler wish to
3077	                             C	; allow processing to continue; 2) the original interrupt return address
3078	                             C	; to the code that performed the function call in the first place. If the
3079	                             C	; user CTRL-C handler wishes to continue, it must leave all registers
 The Microsoft MACRO Assembler             11-06-<6    	PAGE	1-57
MS-DOS version 1.25 by Tim Paterson     March 3, 1982


3080	                             C	; unchanged and IRET. The function that was interrupted will simply be
3081	                             C	; repeated.
